    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d347">

                <pb facs="0287" n="275" xml:id="pg275"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00111A.R.–N.C./22: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Uruguay</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Montevideo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-01-30T17:00:00-03:00">January 30, 1940—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 30—4:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>11. Department’s circular telegram January 27, 3 p.m. I mentioned this matter to
                        Guani<anchor n="29" xml:id="d347-f1"/> today. I have the impression that he
                    is somewhat piqued because Uruguay was not invited to have a representative on
                    the Neutrality Committee. However, I believe that he shares our views regarding
                    the competence of the Committee.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d347-f1" n="29" xml:id="d347-f1-content">
                <p>Alberto Guani, Uruguayan Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
